kosha relative-velocity

  -- velocity wearing an apeksha (reference body) instead of the default
  -- sthira-apeksha (fixed ground frame).
  --
  -- v_A_wrt_B = v_A - v_B
  --
  -- this is not a new physical law — it is the same kramanusara structure
  -- as velocity, but the reference has shifted from the fixed frame to
  -- another moving body. initial-velocity and final-velocity are velocity
  -- wearing an avastha (time-state). relative-velocity is velocity wearing
  -- an apeksha (reference body). the qualifier changes; the arithmetic is
  -- identical: subtraction of two quantities of the same kind.
  --
  -- same time frame (sthira-apeksha shared):
  -- both v_A and v_B are measured wrt the same ground (dt is the same dt).
  -- the time cancels: dx_A/dt - dx_B/dt = d(x_A - x_B)/dt.
  -- relative velocity IS the rate of change of the separation between A and B.
  --
  -- the observer-observed structure:
  -- relative velocity has an asymmetry that most physics quantities do not.
  -- v_A_wrt_B ≠ v_B_wrt_A (they are negatives of each other).
  -- this means there is always a kshetrajna (the observer, the one whose frame
  -- is chosen) and a kshetra (the field being observed from that frame).
  -- the observer is not given by physics — the observer is chosen by the questioner.
  -- "find relative velocity of ball-A wrt ball-B" — ball-A is the kshetrajna,
  -- ball-B is the kshetra. the question names the frame.
  --
  -- this is true of ALL observation in reality:
  -- every measurement is made from somewhere by someone.
  -- sthira-apeksha (fixed ground frame) is just the default kshetrajna —
  -- the implicit observer we forget is a choice.
  -- relative velocity makes the observer explicit. the apeksha is named.
  --
  -- for N entities: always a pairwise question.
  -- you cannot ask "what is the relative velocity of all N entities?"
  -- you must name: whose frame? observed from where?
  -- the mantra takes exactly two roles: observer-velocity and observed-velocity.
  -- "find relative velocity of ball-A wrt ball-B" resolves:
  --   observer = ball-A (scope entity, the frame)
  --   observed = ball-B (the wrt entity)
  --
  -- galilean composition (addition of velocities):
  -- v_A_wrt_C = v_A_wrt_B + v_B_wrt_C
  -- this is the group law of galilean relativity.
  -- a passenger walking at 5 km/h on a train moving at 80 km/h wrt ground:
  -- passenger wrt ground = 5 + 80 = 85 km/h (same direction).
  --
  -- everyday language: "wrt", "relative to", "with respect to",
  -- "as seen from", "in the frame of".

  "linear-motion-varga-vishesa"

  "velocity-abheda"              -- IS velocity in a named reference frame
  "chala-apeksha-sthita"         -- reference body is moving (chala)
  "apeksha-yukta"                -- the reference body is explicitly named
  "sthira-apeksha-yukta"         -- composition reaches back to ground frame
  "kshetrajna-yukta"             -- the observer is always present (even when unnamed)

  -- the group structure of velocity addition
  "group-witness-yukta"          -- galilean addition forms a group
  "addition-kriya"               -- composition rule is addition

done
